C-PHY support in CAMSS: the surya's rear camera speaks in three phases

The surya's rear camera does not deliver a single packet to the CSID, and the
reason is not a badly set option but that transmitter and receiver speak
different languages: the IMX682 is wired in three-lane C-PHY and CAMSS only
knows D-PHY.

THE PROOF, by two independent paths and from the phone itself

    laneAssign      IMX682 = 0x0210 (3)     the other cameras = 0x3210 (4)
    all FIVE vendor mode tables write 0x0114 = 0x02

On a Sony, three lanes is a C-PHY trio.

And it fits EVERYTHING measured on the rear camera, each thing with its control:

    the sensor emits        0x0100 = 0x01, and twelve registers out of twelve
                            match the vendor file
    the PHY hears           from 0 to 13.683 interrupts/s
    the CSID receives none  packets=0, with the front camera marking 2805 in
                            the same session as a positive control

    link rate               8 values from 217 to 870 MHz DDR: nothing
    lanes                   2 and 3: nothing
    settle count            from 20 to 48, covers the standard's window: nothing
    clocks                  the four of csiphy0 at the vendor rates
    supplies                avdd and dvdd rise when transmitting

If the receiver listens in the wrong language, no receiver setting can fix it.
That is why nothing moved a bit.

WHAT WAS NEEDED

  1. camss.c rejected the link at the earliest possible place:
         if (vep.bus_type != V4L2_MBUS_CSI2_DPHY)
                 dev_err(dev, "Unsupported bus type %d\n", ...)
     It now also accepts V4L2_MBUS_CSI2_CPHY and propagates it.

  2. The CSID defines CSI2_RX_CFG0_PHY_TYPE_SEL (bit 24) and NEVER wrote it.
     Measured before the patch: rx_cfg0 = 0x00002102, bit 24 zero.

  3. The CSIPHY needs its three-phase table, which is ANOTHER one: it touches an
     area at 0x900 / 0xA00 / 0xB00 that the D-PHY branch does not use, and sets
     the common mode 0x0800 = 0x0E instead of 0x02. Copied from the phone's own
     Android kernel (csiphy_3ph_v1_2_reg), 3 trios x 30 writes.

  4. In C-PHY the settle count goes in TWO registers -0x010C low and 0x0108
     high- whereas in D-PHY it is one. csiphy_gen2_config_lanes did not handle
     CSIPHY_SETTLE_CNT_HIGHER_BYTE and took a u8, which is not enough for two
     bytes.

  5. The device tree declares bus-type = <MEDIA_BUS_TYPE_CSI2_CPHY> at both ends
     of the rear camera's link.

The table choice is made per LINK and not per SoC, because the same CSIPHY can
serve a D-PHY sensor or a C-PHY one depending on which one it is connected to.
The front camera carries on down the D-PHY branch without noticing.
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss.h
